Description: Make sort's temporary output file with mkstemp and write through it
 When the output file was also an input, sort wrote to OUTPUT.tmp and
 renamed it over the output. It took the first such name that did not
 exist and opened it with fopen, which follows a symbolic link. Another
 user could leave a dangling link under that name in a shared directory,
 and a sort run by root would create or truncate the link's target.
 .
 sort now makes the file with mkstemp in the output's directory, with mode
 0600 as before, and keeps its descriptor. openfile writes the output
 through a duplicate of that descriptor and never opens the file by name,
 so a link put in its place is not followed. sort removes the file if it
 exits with an error or on a signal. rename replaces the output, so the
 output is no longer unlinked first, and a failed rename leaves it as it
 was. The file is not listed with the spill files, so --compress-program
 does not compress the output. With -c or -C sort writes no output, and it
 no longer makes the file or replaces the output with it.
